Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "nigeria": westAfricanCrownStateArticle({
    title:"Nigeria",
    eyebrow:"Federal Crown state · Gulf of Guinea",
    infoboxTitle:"Nigeria",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes that refugee inflows placed Nigeria under acute material pressure and that British withdrawal risked rapid German occupation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Nigeria's development as British West Africa's largest internally self-governing federation, with extensive federal and regional domestic authority beneath continuing Crown and Commonwealth strategic control, is accepted constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its scale, internal diversity, and position between German West Africa and Kamerun make it a central obstacle to German continental consolidation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 -->Severe refugee pressure, inadequate local resources, British reluctance to surrender Nigeria, and the danger of German occupation are direct authorial decisions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Federalization under the 1957 Accra Compact, regional domestic autonomy, Nigerian ministries and armed forces, continued Crown or British defense authority, the Accra Clause, sterling ties, Lagos access, and continued British association after Ghana's realignment are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The formal state title, exact constitution, regional map after 1957, flag, capital settlement, officeholders, elections, military organization, and sovereignty endpoint remain open. Pre-divergence regional and institutional background survives unless displaced.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federation_of_Nigeria",label:"Wikipedia — historical Federation of Nigeria background"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colonial_Nigeria",label:"Wikipedia — colonial and regional background"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"formation",title:"Formation and federal inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>British Nigeria entered the divergence as a political union of sharply different regions and administrative inheritances. The Muslim north, Yoruba west, Igbo east, Lagos, the Niger delta, minority belts, emirates, cities, and customary authorities possessed distinct political and social histories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup></p><p>Survival of those distinctions through Britain's defeat, and their use as the foundation for postwar federal self-government, are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"accra",title:"The Accra federal settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>The 1957 <a href='accra-compact.html'>Accra Compact</a> converting Nigeria into an internally self-governing federation is accepted canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> An African federal ministry and legislature exercise domestic authority while regional governments retain extensive powers over land, education, customary institutions, police, and development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>British reservations over defense, external alignment, strategic bases and communications, currency coordination, and emergency intervention are accepted elements of the settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their precise legal form remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"regions",title:"Regions and constituent authority",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>Northern, Western, and Eastern regional institutions as the initial federal structure, a special federal position for Lagos, and continuing claims for minority protection are accepted constitutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Emirates, chiefly councils, municipal governments, unions, professional bodies, and elected assemblies overlap rather than disappear.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The later regional or state map remains open, but preservation of constituent constitutional rights against simple central-majority abolition is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Government and the Crown",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>Federal responsibility for most taxation, finance, interregional trade, major transport, customs, courts, and common services is accepted administrative development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The Crown or British guarantee retains the defense, alignment, base, communications, and emergency powers fixed by the settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Nigerian territorial citizenship governing franchise, passports, residence, and public office without an unrestricted right of settlement in Britain is accepted under the wider Accra system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Economy and infrastructure",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>Lagos, Port Harcourt, the railways, Niger and Benue waterways, cocoa, groundnuts, palm produce, Plateau tin and columbite, shipping, and a large internal market belong to Nigeria's inherited economic geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their survival and expansion make Nigeria the economic center of Commonwealth West Africa and turn regional revenue disputes into constitutional questions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Commonwealth contracts expanding ports, power, roads, public health, and technical education while preserving British and Dominion commercial leverage are compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact projects and terms remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"society",title:"Religion, language, and public life",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>Northern Islamic emirates and courts, stronger Christian mission and school networks across much of the south, customary institutions, indigenous religious practice, and the use of English alongside Hausa, Yoruba, Igbo, and many other languages belong to inherited Nigerian society.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup></p><p>Federal politics turning language, schooling, civil-service recruitment, and regional representation into questions of national survival is compatible political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"security",title:"Security and the German frontier",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes refugee inflow from German-held territories, Nigeria's strategic importance, and the danger that German power would fill a British withdrawal.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Nigeria serving as a British wedge between German West Africa, Kamerun, and the German Sahel is accepted strategic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Ports, troops, police, airfields, frontier districts, intelligence, and the separate <a href='southern-cameroons.html'>Southern Cameroons</a> buffer supporting that role are compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact forces, facilities, and frontier arrangements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"after-accra",title:"After the Accra Disaster",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n7r3k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:05:00-06:00 --><p>Ghana's 1963 realignment and its use as the warning against unrestricted strategic separation are accepted canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Nigeria thereafter retaining complete or near-complete internal self-government, regional parliaments, Nigerian ministers and armed forces, the Crown, sterling ties, selected Lagos access, defense coordination, and an <a href='accra-clause.html'>Accra Clause</a> is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Nigeria's constitutional position after 1985 and its ultimate right or mechanism to leave the Commonwealth security order remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...westAfricanCrownStatesRelated,{href:"southern-cameroons.html",kicker:"Eastern frontier",label:"Southern Cameroons"},{href:"associated-crown-state.html",kicker:"Related status",label:"Associated Crown State"},{href:"accra-clause.html",kicker:"Post-1963 safeguard",label:"Accra Clause"}],
    facts:[["Conventional name","Nigeria; formal state title open"],["Initial federal capital","Lagos; later arrangement open"],["Accepted government","Internally self-governing federation"],["Constituent structure","Regional governments and local authorities"],["Federal administrative language","English"],["Accepted citizenship","Nigerian territorial citizenship"],["External defense","British Commonwealth"],["Principal ports","Lagos and Port Harcourt"],["Post-1985 status","Open"]]
  }),

  "southern-cameroons": westAfricanCrownStateArticle({
    title:"Southern Cameroons",
    eyebrow:"Crown Frontier Territory · Gulf of Guinea–Cameroon frontier",
    infoboxTitle:"Southern Cameroons",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4c8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:10:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes acute refugee and food pressure in Southern Cameroons and a British fear that withdrawal would permit rapid German occupation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its survival as a fortified Crown Frontier Territory with internal government, local citizenship, Nigerian economic connections, and retained British defense and intelligence facilities is accepted constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its exact postwar boundaries are unresolved because later canon places Buea, historically within British Southern Cameroons, in German Mittelafrika as the Reichskommissariat's ceremonial capital from 1947.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4c8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:10:00-06:00 -->Refugee pressure, inadequate local resources, British strategic concern, and the risk of German occupation are direct authorial decisions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A separate fortified frontier territory after the Accra Compact, with an assembly, local citizenship, Nigerian economic connections, enlarged constabulary, refugee districts, British defense guarantee, permanent air, signals, and intelligence facilities, and proximity to Kribi, is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The surviving sources do not reconcile this settlement with Buea's canonical German status. The postwar border, territorial continuity, capital, official constitutional name, flag, officeholders, parties, elections, relationship with Northern Cameroons, and sovereignty endpoint remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Cameroons",label:"Wikipedia — historical Southern Cameroons background"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"mandate",title:"Mandate and Nigerian administration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4c8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:10:00-06:00 --><p>Before the divergence, British Southern Cameroons formed part of the British-administered former German Kamerun mandate and was governed in close association with Nigeria.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> English-language institutions, trade routes, mission networks, and distinct Cameroonian politics survived into the postwar settlement as accepted background.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The continued name does not establish that the Crown Frontier Territory retained every part of the historical mandate.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"separation",title:"Separation under the Accra Compact",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4c8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:10:00-06:00 --><p>The 1957 settlement removing Southern Cameroons from automatic Nigerian incorporation and establishing it as a <a href='crown-frontier-territory.html'>Crown Frontier Territory</a> is accepted canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its assembly, executive, courts, local citizenship, and authority over land, education, development, and ordinary policing are accepted institutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact constitutional instrument and flag remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Government and citizenship",html:"<p>A territorial ministry governs through elected representatives, chiefs, municipalities, customary authorities, and a professional civil service. Local citizenship distinguishes residence, franchise, and office from Nigerian nationality while preserving regulated movement and commerce across the border.</p><p>Exact electoral and constitutional arrangements remain unsettled in the surviving record.</p>"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"The German frontier",html:"<p>German Cameroon places the territory opposite the Kribi aerospace, signals, naval, and missile complex. Patrol roads, observation posts, airfields, communications stations, and refugee districts make the border one of the most closely watched in Africa.</p><p>The risk of German-backed political action is real, but it also supplies London with a permanent argument against strategic independence.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Economy and Nigerian connection",html:"<p>Plantation agriculture, timber, coastal trade, small ports, road links, and Nigerian markets support the territorial economy. Nigeria remains the principal commercial hinterland even after political separation.</p><p>Commonwealth contracts and security expenditure create employment while making public finance dependent upon the frontier role.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"Language, missions, and identity",html:"<p>English serves government and higher education alongside numerous local languages. Christian missions, chiefly institutions, commercial towns, farmers, and migrant communities form competing political constituencies.</p><p>Public debate divides among advocates of separate Cameroonian identity, closer Nigerian federation, greater Crown association, and eventual accommodation with the German-controlled east.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Constabulary and intelligence state",html:"<p>The enlarged constabulary manages ordinary policing, border screening, refugee movement, and protection of infrastructure. British forces and services retain external defense, strategic aviation, signals collection, and reinforcement.</p><p>Residents criticize a system in which surveillance capacity often grows faster than courts, housing, or representative government.</p>"},
      {id:"position",title:"Constitutional position",html:"<p>Southern Cameroons is neither a Nigerian province, a German client, nor an unrestricted sovereign republic. Its status is the most literal application of the <a href='no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html'>No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine</a>: domestic government beneath an explicit frontier guarantee.</p><p>Whether that arrangement becomes permanent association or a stage toward sovereignty remains open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...westAfricanCrownStatesRelated,{href:"crown-frontier-territory.html",kicker:"Constitutional status",label:"Crown Frontier Territory"},{href:"nigeria.html",kicker:"Economic neighbor",label:"Nigeria"},{href:"mittelafrika.html",kicker:"Opposing frontier",label:"Mittelafrika"}],
    facts:[["Accepted status","Crown Frontier Territory"],["Capital","Open; Buea is under German authority"],["Postwar borders","Open"],["Government","Self-governing territorial ministry and assembly"],["Citizenship","Southern Cameroonian"],["Administrative language","English"],["External defense","United Kingdom"],["Principal strategic neighbor","German Cameroon"],["Economic hinterland","Nigeria"],["Sovereignty endpoint","Open"]]
  }),

  "sierra-leone": westAfricanCrownStateArticle({
    title:"Sierra Leone",
    eyebrow:"Self-governing Crown state · Atlantic West Africa",
    infoboxTitle:"Sierra Leone",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes severe refugee and food pressure in Sierra Leone, insufficient local resources, and the strategic danger of British withdrawal.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Sierra Leone's development as a self-governing Crown state and Freetown's role as the principal British naval, hospital, refugee-processing, and convoy hub on the West African Atlantic after Ghana's realignment are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The inherited Colony–Protectorate division, Krio institutions, chiefly authority, mineral districts, and Commonwealth defense requirements remain important to domestic government.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 -->Refugee pressure, inadequate administrative resources, British reluctance to abandon Sierra Leone, and the risk of German occupation are direct authorial decisions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A self-governing Crown-state trajectory, continued Commonwealth association, Freetown's convoy, naval, hospital, and refugee functions, and its strengthening after Takoradi becomes German-accessible are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact constitution, flag, accession instrument, Crown title, officeholders, elections, party system, military organization, and post-1985 constitutional status remain open. Pre-divergence Colony–Protectorate geography and Freetown's harbor are inherited background.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierra_Leone_Colony_and_Protectorate",label:"Wikipedia — historical Colony and Protectorate background"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierra_Leone",label:"Wikipedia — geography and economic background"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Colony and Protectorate inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Sierra Leone entered the divergence with two political geographies: the Crown Colony around Freetown, shaped by the Krio community and English-derived institutions, and the larger Protectorate governed through chiefs, district administration, and customary law.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup></p><p>Postwar self-government joining these jurisdictions while managing competition between Freetown and the interior is compatible constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact institutions and settlements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"freetown",title:"Freetown and the Atlantic system",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Freetown's deep natural harbor and Atlantic position belong to inherited geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Repair, fuel, hospital, communications, convoy, refugee-transport, and Commonwealth relief functions making it Britain's first major West African harbor on the Cape route are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Strategic investment beyond what local commercial demand alone would support is compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact facilities and spending remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"Crown-state settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Under the Accra system, an African ministry, elected legislature, territorial citizenship, courts, taxation, land administration, education, and ordinary police powers are accepted features of Sierra Leonean self-government.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> British retention of external defense, naval access, strategic communications, and agreed emergency powers is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Political use of Crown-state, associated-state, or dominion formulas is compatible, but the formal constitutional title and instrument remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Diamonds, agriculture, and the port",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Diamonds, iron ore and other minerals, rice and other agriculture, coastal commerce, and port services belong to Sierra Leone's inherited economy.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their postwar continuation, expanded public employment, and disputes over mineral licensing and smuggling among chiefs, companies, communities, the territorial treasury, and Commonwealth security agencies are compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact production, revenue, and disputes remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"society",title:"Krio, Protectorate, and national politics",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Krio education, churches, professions, and Freetown civic institutions, together with Protectorate chiefs, farmers, traders, mission schools, and labor organizations, belong to inherited Sierra Leonean society.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their competition over public employment, revenue, representation, and the legal relation between coast and interior is compatible postwar political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"refugee",title:"Refugee and relief administration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes severe refugee pressure and insufficient local capacity in Sierra Leone.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Freetown as a principal node of the <a href='commonwealth-african-reserve-system.html'>Commonwealth African Reserve System</a>, with warehouses, hospitals, registration personnel, shipping, and inland transport supporting wider emergencies, is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The resulting increase in the port's military and intelligence value is compatible strategic reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"accra",title:"After the loss of Takoradi",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Ghana's 1963 German alignment, the loss of dependable Commonwealth access to Takoradi, and Freetown's increased importance as the western naval and refugee center are accepted canon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Additional naval, signals, air, hospital, and stockpile investment is compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact projects, units, and dates remain open.</p><p>Use of the Accra Disaster against proposals for complete strategic separation is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Mature Commonwealth role",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s1e9k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:12:00-06:00 --><p>Substantial domestic government and a recognizable national political life within the Crown and Commonwealth defense order by 1985 are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Sierra Leonean governments using Freetown's importance to seek investment and consultation is compatible political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Whether strategic importance protects the state or limits its freedom of alignment is an unresolved political question; the post-1985 constitutional endpoint remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...westAfricanCrownStatesRelated,{href:"gambia.html",kicker:"Western Atlantic partner",label:"The Gambia"},{href:"ghana.html",kicker:"Former eastern partner",label:"Republic of Ghana"},{href:"commonwealth-african-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Freetown relief network",label:"Commonwealth African Reserve System"}],
    facts:[["Capital","Freetown"],["Accepted status","Self-governing Crown state"],["Government","Territorial ministry and legislature"],["Citizenship","Sierra Leonean territorial citizenship"],["Administrative language","English"],["Strategic asset","Freetown harbor"],["External defense","British Commonwealth"],["Principal exports","Minerals and agricultural produce"],["Mature position","Commonwealth-associated by 1985"],["Post-1985 status","Open"]]
  }),

  "gambia": westAfricanCrownStateArticle({
    title:"The Gambia",
    eyebrow:"Defended Crown protectorate · Gambia River",
    infoboxTitle:"The Gambia",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes severe refugee and food pressure in the Gambia, insufficient local resources, and the danger that German power would follow British withdrawal.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its development as a self-governing British protectorate and Crown-associated river corridor, nearly enclosed by German-controlled Senegal and dependent upon maritime and air support, is accepted constitutional and strategic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Britain retains the river, harbor, and communications position as part of the West African approach to the Cape route.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 -->Refugee pressure, inadequate administrative capacity, British reluctance to abandon the territory, and the risk of German occupation are direct authorial decisions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A defended protectorate and river corridor, German Senegalese enclosure, Royal Navy and air dependence, expanded local government, Atlantic and relief functions, and continued Commonwealth association are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The formal post-1957 constitution, flag, use of Bathurst or Banjul, officeholders, elections, citizenship statute, garrison, post-1985 economy, and sovereignty endpoint remain open. The pre-divergence colony-and-protectorate distinction and river geography are inherited background.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gambia_Colony_and_Protectorate",label:"Wikipedia — historical Gambia Colony and Protectorate background"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gambia",label:"Wikipedia — geographic background"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"A state made by a river",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>The territory's narrow course along both banks of the Gambia River from the Atlantic into the interior, with the Colony around Bathurst and the wider Protectorate, belongs to inherited geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> German control of Senegal turns that corridor into a nearly enclosed British position.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>River transport linking agricultural districts to the coast, together with exposure of roads, settlements, and communications to blockade or frontier pressure, is compatible strategic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Colony and Protectorate",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>The British distinction between the coastal colony and inland protectorate, and the coexistence of municipal government, chiefs, district authorities, traders, farmers, and river communities with unequal access to education, courts, and representation, belong to the pre-divergence inheritance.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their postwar constitutional development remains open except where the Accra settlement establishes wider local government.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"settlement",title:"Post-Accra constitutional settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>An expanded elected legislature, local cabinet, territorial citizenship, land and agricultural powers, and control of ordinary policing are accepted elements of the post-Accra settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> British retention of defense, foreign relations, strategic communications, river access, and emergency reinforcement is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Protectorate, Crown-state, and associated-territory formulas remain compatible political usages; the formal constitutional title and instrument are open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"fortress",title:"The defended river corridor",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>Bathurst harbor, coastal aviation, signals, river patrol, and guarded-depot functions are accepted features of the defended corridor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The territory's inability to resist a determined attack alone, coupled with a British guarantee that raises the cost of seizure, is accepted strategic synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact facilities, units, and response plans remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Groundnuts, river trade, and dependence",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>Groundnut production, river transport, trading houses, markets, port services, and dependence on imported food belong to the inherited Gambian economy.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Continued reliance on shipping, British finance, and Commonwealth procurement, with exposure to route disruption and administered prices, is compatible postwar development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact trade and price arrangements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"senegal",title:"The German Senegal frontier",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>German control of Senegal around most Gambian land approaches is accepted postwar geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Smuggling, family movement, livestock, labor migration, refugees, and intelligence traffic across a boundary difficult to seal are compatible frontier development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Application of the <a href='no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html'>No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine</a> to this exposed territory is accepted institutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"society",title:"Government and society",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>English administration, Mandinka, Fula, Wolof, Jola, Serer, and other languages, a Muslim majority, and smaller Christian and other communities belong to inherited Gambian society.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Competition among Bathurst professionals and traders, protectorate chiefs, farmers, religious authorities, and younger nationalists is compatible postwar politics.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Mature status",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g6m2k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:15:00-06:00 --><p>Continued domestic self-government and strategic dependence after Ghana's realignment are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> British protection and infrastructure in exchange for bases, communications access, and limits on independent alignment are compatible constitutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact agreements and investments remain open.</p><p>A sovereign Commonwealth realm, associated republic, permanent Crown protectorate, or another later formula remains possible; the sovereignty endpoint is unresolved.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...westAfricanCrownStatesRelated,{href:"sierra-leone.html",kicker:"Nearest British Atlantic hub",label:"Sierra Leone"},{href:"commonwealth-african-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Relief connection",label:"Commonwealth African Reserve System"},{href:"no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html",kicker:"Strategic justification",label:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine"}],
    facts:[["Capital","Bathurst; later name open"],["Accepted status","Self-governing Crown protectorate"],["Government","Local cabinet and legislature"],["Citizenship","Gambian territorial citizenship"],["Administrative language","English"],["Geography","Gambia River corridor"],["Land neighbor","German Senegal"],["External defense","United Kingdom"],["Principal export","Groundnuts"],["Sovereignty endpoint","Open"]]
  }),

  "ghana": westAfricanCrownStateArticle({
    title:"Republic of Ghana",
    eyebrow:"Sovereign republic · German-associated West Africa",
    infoboxTitle:"Ghana",
    flag:westAfricanGhanaFlag,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes a rapid revolutionary collapse after Labour grants complete sovereignty to an apparently stable West African colony, followed by close German alignment under entirely African victors.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The accepted setting identifies the state as the Republic of Ghana, independent from 6 March 1963 and governed after the Accra Disaster by the National Liberation Directorate.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Ghana remains legally sovereign while the Accra–Lomé relationship gives Germany extensive leverage over arms, credit, communications, cocoa exports, and Takoradi development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 -->A 1960s West African independence grant, loss of British intervention authority, rapid victory by entirely African revolutionaries, close post-victory German alignment, lasting Labour trauma, and the discrediting of unrestricted sovereignty are direct authorial decisions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Selection of the Gold Coast, the 1957 Associated Crown State, Ghanaian independence on 6 March 1963, republican status, the Accra Disaster, National Liberation Directorate, Accra–Lomé Treaty, German military and economic support, Takoradi access, cocoa dependence, and continuing Ghanaian legal sovereignty are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formal Einheitspakt status, heads of state, ministers, exact constitution, regional boundaries, elections, later coups, population, armed-force size, and domestic development after 1963 remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    additionalSources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Coast_(British_colony)",label:"Wikipedia — historical Gold Coast background"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana",label:"Wikipedia — geographic and social background"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"gold-coast",title:"The Gold Coast inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>The coastal Gold Coast colony, Ashanti, Northern Territories, and British Togoland, with Accra, Kumasi, Takoradi, and distinct northern political and economic geography, belong to the inherited territorial structure.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their combination in the postwar Gold Coast and Republic of Ghana is accepted continuity.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Cocoa, gold, timber, other minerals, railways, ports, schools, missions, chiefly institutions, and an experienced civil service making the territory Britain's strongest initial candidate for self-government is accepted constitutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"associated",title:"Associated Crown State, 1957–1963",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>The Gold Coast as the public model of the 1957 <a href='accra-compact.html'>Accra Compact</a> is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> An African ministry and legislature controlling domestic policy, citizenship, courts, land, education, agriculture, taxation, and ordinary police while Britain retains defense, external alignment, Takoradi access, and emergency powers is the accepted settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Nationalist acceptance of internal government alongside continued demands that British reservations expire is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Parties, leaders, and exact constitutional provisions remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Cocoa, minerals, and regional distribution",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>African cocoa farming and marketing, gold and other mining, timber, railways, urban commerce, and Takoradi shipping belong to the inherited Gold Coast economy.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Postwar disputes over producer prices, export revenue, concessions, regional investment, and central control, including their connection to Ashanti and northern constitutional claims, are accepted political-economic reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact policies, prices, and revenues remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"independence",title:"Ghana Independence Act",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes a Labour grant of complete sovereignty that removes Britain's legal authority to intervene.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <a href='ghana-independence-act.html'>Ghana Independence Act</a>, termination of Crown sovereignty, reserve powers, external-defense control, and most permanent base rights on 6 March 1963, and creation of a republic are accepted legal development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>A brief transitional mission without a permanent defense treaty, intended to demonstrate undiluted independence, is accepted reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact statutory and transitional terms remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"disaster",title:"The Accra Disaster",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes rapid revolutionary collapse, victory by an entirely African movement after a short campaign, and British non-intervention under the independence settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Ghana's seven-month interval, centralist, Ashanti, northern, refugee, labor, cocoa, military, and traditional-authority disputes, roughly one hundred days of war, evacuation of the constitutional government, and National Liberation Directorate victory are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Commanders, units, casualties, and tactical sequence remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"treaty",title:"Accra–Lomé alignment",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>Close German alignment after the African revolutionary victory is directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <a href='accra-lome-treaty.html'>Accra–Lomé Treaty</a>, German recognition, arms, advisers, credit, cocoa purchases, communications, infrastructure, and influence over procurement, encryption, exports, intelligence, and Takoradi are accepted treaty development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Ghanaian legal sovereignty alongside contractual, financial, military, and technical dependence is accepted client-state synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formal Einheitspakt membership, treaty text, access rights, quantities, and command arrangements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Revolutionary government and sovereignty",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>The National Liberation Directorate as a Ghanaian coalition governing through national symbols, citizenship, courts, administration, and armed forces is accepted institutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its presentation of German support as assistance to an African revolution, and opponents' description of sovereignty constrained by dependence on German weapons, credit, and communications, are accepted political positions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The Directorate's exact constitution, membership, and later history remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"international",title:"International position",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g8h4k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T07:20:00-06:00 --><p>Ghana providing Germany with an Atlantic political and logistical foothold and interrupting Britain's West African chain between Sierra Leone and Nigeria is accepted strategic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> German presentation of Ghana as a sovereign African ally, and Commonwealth use of the crisis to justify the <a href='accra-clause.html'>Accra Clause</a>, are accepted propaganda and constitutional consequences.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>The event's use by African nationalists as evidence that alternatives to British protection exist, together with disputes over the inequality of German alignment, is compatible political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ghana-accra.html",kicker:"Sequence overview",label:"Ghana and the Accra Disaster"},{href:"ghana-independence-act.html",kicker:"Sovereignty transfer",label:"Ghana Independence Act"},{href:"accra-disaster.html",kicker:"Founding civil conflict",label:"Accra Disaster"},{href:"accra-lome-treaty.html",kicker:"German alignment",label:"Accra–Lomé Treaty"},{href:"british-africa.html",kicker:"Former regional system",label:"British Africa"},{href:"mittelafrika.html",kicker:"Principal external patron",label:"Mittelafrika"}],
    facts:[["Capital","Accra"],["Government","Revolutionary republic; exact constitution open"],["Accepted independence","6 March 1963"],["Accepted predecessor","Gold Coast Associated Crown State"],["Accepted ruling authority after 1963","National Liberation Directorate"],["Principal port","Takoradi"],["Principal export","Cocoa"],["Strategic alignment","German-aligned client; formal bloc status open"],["Former defense power","United Kingdom"],["Later domestic chronology","Open"]]
  })
});

function appendWestAfricanCrownStates(id,key,items){
  const article=window.deepArticles[id];
  if(!article)return;
  article[key]=article[key]||[];
  for(const item of items){
    if(!article[key].some(existing=>(existing.id&&existing.id===item.id)||(existing.href&&existing.href===item.href))) article[key].push(item);
  }
}

